      <div class="sit-label">The Situation</div>
      <div class="sit-grid">
        <div class="sit-item"><span class="sit-val">2500</span><span class="sit-key">Height (ft)</span></div>
        <div class="sit-item"><span class="sit-val">−450</span><span class="sit-key">Speed (ft/turn)</span></div>
        <div class="sit-item"><span class="sit-val">600</span><span class="sit-key">Fuel (units)</span></div>
        <div class="sit-item"><span class="sit-val">−5</span><span class="sit-key">Gravity (ft/turn²)</span></div>
      </div>
      <p class="sit-desc">Each turn you choose how much fuel to <strong>burn</strong> (0–75 units). Burning fuel slows your descent. Run out of fuel and gravity wins. <strong>To win:</strong> reach height = 0 with speed ≥ −5 ft/turn.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="steps" id="steps-container">

      <!-- Step 1 -->
      <div class="step-card" id="step-1">
        <div class="step-header" onclick="toggleStep(1)">
          <div class="step-left">
            <span class="step-num">01</span>
            <div>
              <div class="step-title">Variables — Remembering Things</div>
              <div class="step-concept">Variables · Assignment · f-strings</div>
            </div>
          </div>
          <span class="step-chevron" id="chev-1">▼</span>
        </div>
        <div class="step-body" id="body-1">
          <div class="why-box">
            <strong>Why do we need this?</strong> The computer has no memory of its own. You have to give everything a name. We need to remember four things about the lander: height, speed, fuel, and how strong gravity is.
          </div>
          <div class="code-block"><pre><code># The starting state of the lander
height  = 2500   # feet above the surface
speed   = -450   # ft/turn (negative = falling)
fuel    = 600    # fuel units
gravity = -5     # ft/turn (always pulling down)

print("Lander ready.")
print(f"Height: {height} ft")
print(f"Speed:  {speed} ft/turn")
print(f"Fuel:   {fuel} units")</code></pre></div>
          <div class="try-box">
            <strong>Try it:</strong> Change <code>height</code> to <code>500</code> and re-run. What changes? Change it back.
          </div>
          <div class="concept-tag">💡 <code>f"...{variable}..."</code> is an <strong>f-string</strong> — it puts the value of a variable directly inside text. The <code>=</code> sign stores a value into a named variable.</div>
          <button class="btn-done" onclick="markDone(1)">✓ Done — next step</button>
        </div>
      </div>

      <!-- Step 2 -->
      <div class="step-card" id="step-2">
        <div class="step-header" onclick="toggleStep(2)">
          <div class="step-left">
            <span class="step-num">02</span>
            <div>
              <div class="step-title">Physics — One Turn of the Game</div>
              <div class="step-concept">Arithmetic · Order of operations · Assignment</div>
            </div>
          </div>
          <span class="step-chevron" id="chev-2">▼</span>
        </div>
        <div class="step-body" id="body-2">
          <div class="why-box">
            <strong>Why do we need this?</strong> Every turn, three things happen in a fixed order: you burn fuel, your speed changes, your height changes. This is the entire physics engine — three lines.
          </div>
          <div class="physics-box">
            <div class="physics-eq">fuel = fuel − burn</div>
            <div class="physics-eq">speed = speed + burn + gravity</div>
            <div class="physics-eq">height = height + speed</div>
          </div>
          <p style="font-size:13px;color:var(--muted)">Burn pushes up (+). Gravity pulls down (−5 every turn). Speed accumulates. Height follows speed. The order matters — speed must update before height.</p>
          <div class="code-block"><pre><code># Reset the lander
height  = 2500
speed   = -450
fuel    = 600
gravity = -5

# --- Turn 1: burn 50 units ---
burn = 50

fuel   = fuel - burn
speed  = speed + burn + gravity
height = height + speed

print(f"After turn 1 (burn={burn}):")
print(f"  Speed:  {speed} ft/turn")
print(f"  Height: {height} ft")
print(f"  Fuel:   {fuel} units")</code></pre></div>
          <div class="try-box">
            <strong>Try it:</strong> Change <code>burn = 50</code> to <code>burn = 0</code>. What happens to speed? Now try <code>burn = 75</code>. What's the maximum improvement to speed per turn?
          </div>
          <div class="concept-tag">💡 <code>speed = speed + burn + gravity</code> — the right side is evaluated first, then stored into the variable on the left. This is how Python updates a value.</div>
          <button class="btn-done" onclick="markDone(2)">✓ Done — next step</button>
        </div>
      </div>

      <!-- Step 3 -->
      <div class="step-card" id="step-3">
        <div class="step-header" onclick="toggleStep(3)">
          <div class="step-left">
            <span class="step-num">03</span>
            <div>
              <div class="step-title">Conditionals — Is the Game Over?</div>
              <div class="step-concept">if / elif / else · Comparison operators · Functions</div>
            </div>
          </div>
          <span class="step-chevron" id="chev-3">▼</span>
        </div>
        <div class="step-body" id="body-3">
          <div class="why-box">
            <strong>Why do we need this?</strong> After every turn we need to check: did we land safely? Did we crash? Are we still flying? Python's <code>if/elif/else</code> lets us test conditions in order — first match wins.
          </div>
          <div class="code-block"><pre><code>def check_status(height, speed, fuel):
    """Check the current state. Returns a string describing what happened."""
    if height > 0:
        if fuel &lt;= 0:
            return f"OUT OF FUEL! Height={height}, Speed={speed}."
        else:
            return f"Flying. Height={height} ft, Speed={speed} ft/turn, Fuel={fuel}"
    elif height == 0:
        if speed >= -5:
            return f"SAFE LANDING! Speed={speed} ft/turn."
        else:
            return f"CRASH! Speed={speed} — needed >= -5."
    else:  # height &lt; 0
        depth = abs(height)
        return f"CRASH! Buried {depth} ft into the lunar surface."

# Test each case
print(check_status(500,  -100, 200))   # flying
print(check_status(0,    -3,   10))    # safe landing
print(check_status(0,    -50,  0))     # crash
print(check_status(-20,  -80,  0))     # went below surface</code></pre></div>
          <div class="try-box">
            <strong>Try it:</strong> Call <code>check_status(0, -5, 0)</code>. Is speed −5 a safe landing? What about <code>check_status(0, -6, 0)</code>?
          </div>
          <div class="concept-tag">💡 <code>def</code> defines a reusable function. <code>abs()</code> returns the absolute value. <code>&gt;=</code> means "greater than or equal to".</div>
          <button class="btn-done" onclick="markDone(3)">✓ Done — next step</button>
        </div>
      </div>

      <!-- Step 4 -->
      <div class="step-card" id="step-4">
        <div class="step-header" onclick="toggleStep(4)">
          <div class="step-left">
            <span class="step-num">04</span>
            <div>
              <div class="step-title">The Game Loop — Making it Playable</div>
              <div class="step-concept">while loops · input() · break · continue · int()</div>
            </div>
          </div>
          <span class="step-chevron" id="chev-4">▼</span>
        </div>
        <div class="step-body" id="body-4">
          <div class="why-box">
            <strong>Why do we need this?</strong> A game loop repeats the same steps over and over: show state → get input → update physics → check if over → repeat. Python's <code>while</code> loop keeps going until we tell it to stop with <code>break</code>.
          </div>
          <div class="code-block"><pre><code>def play():
    """Play the Lunar Lander game."""
    height  = 2500
    speed   = -450
    fuel    = 600
    gravity = -5

    print("LUNAR LANDER")
    print("Enter burn amount (0-75) each turn. 'q' to quit.")
    print("Goal: reach height=0 with speed >= -5")
    print("-" * 45)

    while True:
        # Show current state
        print(f"Height: {height:6}  Speed: {speed:6}  Fuel: {fuel:4}")

        # Check if game is over
        if height &lt;= 0:
            if speed >= -5:
                print(f"SAFE LANDING! Speed: {speed}")
            else:
                depth = abs(height)
                print(f"CRASH! Made a crater {depth} ft deep.")
            break

        if fuel &lt;= 0:
            print(f"OUT OF FUEL! Gravity takes over. Speed={speed}")
            # Fall with no burn until impact
            while height > 0:
                speed  = speed + gravity
                height = height + speed
            if speed >= -5:
                print(f"Lucky! Safe landing at speed {speed}")
            else:
                print(f"CRASH! Impact speed: {speed}")
            break

        # Get player input
        raw = input("Burn (0-75): ").strip()
        if raw.lower() == 'q':
            print("Mission aborted.")
            break
        if not raw.isdigit():
            print("  Enter a number 0-75.")
            continue

        burn = int(raw)
        if burn &lt; 0 or burn > 75:
            print("  Burn must be 0-75.")
            continue

        burn = min(burn, fuel)   # can't burn more than you have

        # Apply physics
        fuel   = fuel - burn
        speed  = speed + burn + gravity
        height = height + speed


play()</code></pre></div>
          <div class="try-box">
            <strong>Play it.</strong> Try burning 0 every turn. Try 75 every turn. Find a strategy that works. Hint: you start falling at 450 ft/turn and gravity adds −5 every turn. You need to burn enough to bring that speed up to −5 or better by the time you reach zero.
          </div>
          <div class="concept-tag">💡 <code>while True</code> runs forever until <code>break</code>. <code>continue</code> skips back to the top of the loop. <code>int()</code> converts a string like <code>"50"</code> to the number <code>50</code>. <code>min(a, b)</code> returns the smaller of two values.</div>
          <button class="btn-done" onclick="markDone(4)">✓ Done — next step</button>
        </div>
      </div>

      <!-- Step 5 -->
      <div class="step-card" id="step-5">
        <div class="step-header" onclick="toggleStep(5)">
          <div class="step-left">
            <span class="step-num">05</span>
            <div>
              <div class="step-title">Functions — Tidy It Up</div>
              <div class="step-concept">def · Parameters · Return values · Constants</div>
            </div>
          </div>
          <span class="step-chevron" id="chev-5">▼</span>
        </div>
        <div class="step-body" id="body-5">
          <div class="why-box">
            <strong>Why do we need this?</strong> The game loop works but it's one big block. When code gets long, we split it into functions — each one does one thing and can be tested separately. We also pull out the magic numbers into named constants so they're easy to find and change.
          </div>
          <div class="code-block"><pre><code># ── Constants ─────────────────────────────────────
GRAVITY    = -5
BURN_MAX   = 75
SAFE_SPEED = -5    # must land at speed >= this

# ── One turn of physics ───────────────────────────
def apply_physics(height, speed, fuel, burn):
    """Apply one turn. Returns (height, speed, fuel)."""
    burn   = max(0, min(BURN_MAX, burn))  # clamp to 0..75
    burn   = min(burn, fuel)              # can't burn more than we have
    fuel   = fuel - burn
    speed  = speed + burn + GRAVITY
    height = height + speed
    return height, speed, fuel

# ── Landing checks ─────────────────────────────────
def is_safe_landing(height, speed):
    return height &lt;= 0 and speed >= SAFE_SPEED

def is_crash(height, speed):
    return height &lt;= 0 and speed &lt; SAFE_SPEED

# ── Test it ────────────────────────────────────────
h, s, f = apply_physics(2500, -450, 600, burn=50)
print(f"After burn=50: height={h}, speed={s}, fuel={f}")

print(is_safe_landing(0, -5))   # True
print(is_safe_landing(0, -6))   # False
print(is_safe_landing(100, -3)) # False — still flying</code></pre></div>
          <div class="try-box">
            <strong>Try it:</strong> Call <code>apply_physics(2500, -450, 10, burn=75)</code>. What is the fuel result? Why?
          </div>
          <div class="concept-tag">💡 Functions with multiple return values use a tuple: <code>return a, b, c</code>. Unpack with <code>h, s, f = ...</code>. <code>UPPER_CASE</code> names signal constants — values we never change after setting.</div>
          <button class="btn-done" onclick="markDone(5)">✓ Done — next step</button>
        </div>
      </div>

      <!-- Step 6 -->
      <div class="step-card" id="step-6">
        <div class="step-header" onclick="toggleStep(6)">
          <div class="step-left">
            <span class="step-num">06</span>
            <div>
              <div class="step-title">Automated Tests — Verifying the Physics</div>
              <div class="step-concept">assert · Test functions · Bug hunting</div>
            </div>
          </div>
          <span class="step-chevron" id="chev-6">▼</span>
        </div>
        <div class="step-body" id="body-6">
          <div class="why-box">
            <strong>Why do we need this?</strong> How do you know your physics is correct? You could play the game and hope — or you could write code that checks it for you automatically. If a test fails, you know exactly what's broken.
          </div>
          <div class="code-block"><pre><code>def test_gravity_pulls_down():
    h, s, f = apply_physics(2500, -450, 600, burn=0)
    assert s &lt; -450, "Speed should be more negative (faster fall)"
    assert h &lt; 2500, "Height should decrease"
    print("PASS: test_gravity_pulls_down")

def test_burn_slows_descent():
    _, s0, _ = apply_physics(2500, -450, 600, burn=0)
    _, s1, _ = apply_physics(2500, -450, 600, burn=75)
    assert s1 > s0, "Full burn should give less negative speed than no burn"
    print("PASS: test_burn_slows_descent")

def test_fuel_decreases():
    _, _, f = apply_physics(2500, -450, 600, burn=50)
    assert f == 550, f"Expected 550 fuel, got {f}"
    print("PASS: test_fuel_decreases")

def test_cant_burn_more_than_fuel():
    _, _, f = apply_physics(2500, -450, 10, burn=75)
    assert f == 0, "Fuel should hit zero, not go negative"
    print("PASS: test_cant_burn_more_than_fuel")

def test_safe_landing():
    assert is_safe_landing(0, -5)  == True
    assert is_safe_landing(0, -6)  == False
    assert is_safe_landing(0,  0)  == True
    assert is_safe_landing(100, -3) == False
    print("PASS: test_safe_landing")

# Run all tests
test_gravity_pulls_down()
test_burn_slows_descent()
test_fuel_decreases()
test_cant_burn_more_than_fuel()
test_safe_landing()
print("\nAll tests passed.")</code></pre></div>
          <div class="try-box">
            <strong>Break a test on purpose:</strong> Change <code>GRAVITY = -5</code> to <code>GRAVITY = 5</code> at the top of Step 5 and re-run these tests. Which ones fail and why? Change it back.
          </div>
          <div class="concept-tag">💡 <code>assert condition, "message"</code> raises an error if the condition is false. The <code>_</code> variable is a convention for "I don't need this return value".</div>
          <button class="btn-done" onclick="markDone(6)">✓ Done — next step</button>
        </div>
      </div>

      <!-- Step 7 -->
      <div class="step-card step-challenge" id="step-7">
        <div class="step-header" onclick="toggleStep(7)">
          <div class="step-left">
            <span class="step-num">07</span>
            <div>
              <div class="step-title">Autopilot Challenge 🏆</div>
              <div class="step-concept">Functions as arguments · Control logic · Simulation</div>
            </div>
          </div>
          <span class="step-chevron" id="chev-7">▼</span>
        </div>
        <div class="step-body" id="body-7">
          <div class="challenge-banner">No ceiling. Start simple — go as far as you want.</div>
          <div class="why-box">
            <strong>The challenge:</strong> Write a function that decides the burn each turn automatically. It receives the current state and returns a burn amount (0–75). Make it land safely using as little fuel as possible.
          </div>

          <div class="code-block"><pre><code">def simulate(strategy):
    """
    Run the lander using an autopilot strategy function.
    strategy(turn, height, speed, fuel) -> burn amount
    Returns list of (height, speed, fuel) per turn.
    """
    height, speed, fuel = 2500, -450, 600
    history = [(height, speed, fuel)]
    for turn in range(1, 500):
        burn = strategy(turn, height, speed, fuel)
        height, speed, fuel = apply_physics(height, speed, fuel, burn)
        history.append((height, speed, fuel))
        if height &lt;= 0:
            break
    return history


# ── Your autopilot goes here ───────────────────────
def autopilot(turn, height, speed, fuel):
    """Replace this stub with something that actually lands safely."""
    return 30   # burns 30 every turn — probably won't work!


# ── Run it ─────────────────────────────────────────
run = simulate(autopilot)
final_h, final_s, final_f = run[-1]
print(f"Turns taken: {len(run) - 1}")
print(f"Final speed: {final_s}")
print(f"Fuel left:   {final_f}")
print("SAFE LANDING" if is_safe_landing(final_h, final_s) else "CRASH")</code></pre></div>

          <div class="hints-container">
            <div class="hint-item" id="hint1">
              <button class="hint-toggle" onclick="toggleHint('hint1')">💡 Hint 1 — Think about what speed you want</button>
              <div class="hint-body" style="display:none">You need speed ≥ −5 when you hit the ground. If speed is much more negative than that, burn hard. If you're already close to safe speed, ease off or coast.</div>
            </div>
            <div class="hint-item" id="hint2">
              <button class="hint-toggle" onclick="toggleHint('hint2')">💡 Hint 2 — A target speed that scales with height</button>
              <div class="hint-body" style="display:none">Try: <code>target = -(height // 100)</code>. At height 2500 that's −25. At height 100 it's −1. It gets gentler as you approach. If <code>speed &lt; target</code>, burn. Otherwise coast.</div>
            </div>
            <div class="hint-item" id="hint3">
              <button class="hint-toggle" onclick="toggleHint('hint3')">💡 Hint 3 — How much to burn?</button>
              <div class="hint-body" style="display:none">The gap between your current speed and your target speed is the <em>error</em>. <code>error = target - speed</code>. If error is large, burn a lot. If small, burn a little. Try: <code>burn = min(75, max(0, error))</code>.</div>
            </div>
          </div>

          <div class="autopilot-tests">
            <div class="at-label">Your autopilot must pass these tests:</div>
            <div class="code-block"><pre><code">def test_autopilot_lands():
    run = simulate(autopilot)
    h, s, f = run[-1]
    assert h &lt;= 0, "Must reach the surface"
    print("PASS: lands")

def test_autopilot_safe():
    run = simulate(autopilot)
    h, s, f = run[-1]
    assert is_safe_landing(h, s), f"CRASH at speed {s} — needed >= -5"
    print("PASS: safe landing")

def test_autopilot_efficient():
    run = simulate(autopilot)
    h, s, f = run[-1]
    fuel_used = 600 - f
    pct = fuel_used / 600 * 100
    assert pct &lt; 90, f"Used {pct:.0f}% of fuel — try to use less"
    print(f"PASS: efficient ({pct:.0f}% fuel used)")

test_autopilot_lands()
test_autopilot_safe()
test_autopilot_efficient()</code></pre></div>
          </div>
